# HED-FLORAL — Next Floral + Wood/Amber Structures — Recommendations

**Date:** 2026-09-02  
**Inventory basis:** live `inventory.txt`  
**Essential-oil policy:** the EO prohibition applies only to the Rose de Mai formula; EOs are selected below when they have a target-linked function  
**Authority:** architecture shortlist only; beauty, liking, performance, safety, stability, and release are NOT TESTED

These are ranked by the quality of the floral-to-wood/amber relationship, separation from the Rose/Hyacinth/Magnolia–Peony set, live-stock plausibility, and the clarity of the smallest resolving comparison.

## 1. Orange Blossom Resin Gold

**Floral core:** Neroli EO 10% in DPG + Petitgrain EO Paraguay + Aurantiol neat + Oranger Crystals 10% in DPG.  
**Amber structure:** Labdanum Resinoid 10% in DPG + Siam Benzoin 50% in DPG; Azarbre only as a later dry counterweight.

The orange-flower body should cast a warm resin afterimage rather than sit on top of a separate amber base. This is the strongest next development because the honeyed floral-to-balsam transition is native and it does not duplicate the three finished florals.

**Failure mode:** grape candy from the orange-flower materials; syrup or generic amber from too much Benzoin/Labdanum.  
**First test:** fixed orange-blossom core, resin module absent versus present at constant total. Keep Azarbre out until the resin relation earns retention.

## 2. Jasmine Cedar Veil

**Floral core:** Jasmine Absolute 10% in DPG + Benzyl Acetate + Cis Jasmone or Dihydrojasmone; Ylang Comoros III EO as a restrained petal-thickness modifier; optional Indole 10% only after the clean core works.  
**Wood/amber structure:** Cedramber as the main seam, with low Kephalis as the darker alternative; Amberwood F only as a terminal anchor.

Cedramber can turn the jasmine's dry floral shadow into a continuous cedar veil. Kephalis is the high-character branch if a darker tobacco-amber undertone is wanted.

**Failure mode:** abstract woody perfume, tobacco takeover, or fecal pressure if Indole and Kephalis are stacked too early.  
**First test:** identical jasmine core over Cedramber, with versus without one low Kephalis intervention.

## 3. Violet Cedar Paper

**Floral core:** Alpha Ionone + restrained Beta Ionone + Alpha Isomethyl Ionone; Ultralia only if the violet needs a low ghost note.  
**Wood/amber structure:** Cedramber versus Timberol; Amberwood F is optional after the main wood choice.

The violet/ionone family and dry cedar materials can form a coherent pencil-wood, petal, and paper continuum. It is the most elegant dry option in the set.

**Failure mode:** redundant ionone powder or bare lumber.  
**First test:** one fixed violet core, Cedramber arm versus Timberol arm. Do not stack both woods in the first pass.

## 4. Mimosa Cashmere Amber

**Floral core:** Mimosa Absolute 10% in DPG + Heliotropal neat + trace Anisaldehyde 10% + Alpha Isomethyl Ionone; Blue Chamomile EO only as a later diluted hay/tobacco-shadow experiment.  
**Wood/amber structure:** Cashmeran 20% + Azarbre; Ambermax 50% only as a later projection experiment.

Mimosa's pollen, honey, and powder can pass naturally through violet powder into Cashmeran textile warmth and rounded Azarbre cedar-amber.

**Failure mode:** marzipan, uniform beige powder, or Ambermax takeover.  
**First test:** fixed Mimosa–Cashmeran–Azarbre core without versus with the minimum practical Ambermax intervention.

## 5. Gardenia Lacquered Sandalwood

**Floral core:** Stralyl Acetate + DBCA + restrained ACA + Benzyl Salicylate + a low Ylang Comoros III EO natural-petal layer.  
**Wood structure:** choose either Ebanol or Bacdanol, not both; Sandalwood EO 10% in DPG may bridge the chosen molecule into a more natural wood surface; Azarbre only if the sandalwood is too soft.

The gardenia materials make a green-sweet waxy petal, while Benzyl Salicylate forms the lacquer seam into creamy sandalwood. Ebanol gives the silkier version; Bacdanol gives the rounder, milkier version.

**Failure mode:** plastic cosmetic white floral, suntan lotion, or sandalwood replacing the flower.  
**First test:** one gardenia core with Ebanol versus the same core with Bacdanol, OAV-matched and constant total.

## 6. Osmanthus Suede Amber

**Floral core:** Osmanthus Absolute 10% in DPG + optional Beta Ionone 0.1% in TEC; Damascone Beta 10% only if the apricot facet needs a controlled reflector.  
**Wood/amber structure:** Suederal 10% + Cabreuva EO 50% w/w in DPG + Azarbre + restrained Amberwood F.

This is a highly congruent relationship: Suederal catches the absolute's own apricot-leather ambiguity, while Azarbre and Amberwood F extend it into a dry warm residue.

**Failure mode:** leather becomes the subject; Damascone turns apricot into plum/Rose; warm amber erases tea delicacy.  
**First test:** fixed Osmanthus–Azarbre core without versus with one low Suederal intervention.

## 7. Tuberose Night Lacquer

**Floral core:** Tuberose Absolute 10% in DPG with restrained Benzyl Acetate and Ylang Comoros III EO; opaque Tuberose/Tuberlia bases remain optional and require their own authority decision.  
**Wood structure:** Bacdanol + Black Agarwood Artificial 10% w/w in DPG; no Ambermax in the first pass.

Bacdanol receives the creamy flower while the artificial agarwood exposes a darker animalic/smoky reflection. This is the highest-contrast and highest-risk option.

**Failure mode:** medicinal/rubbery wood, coconut cream, or the dark wood replacing tuberose.  
**First test:** fixed Tuberose–Bacdanol core without versus with the minimum practical artificial-agarwood intervention.

## Development Order

Advance these first:

1. Orange Blossom Resin Gold.
2. Jasmine Cedar Veil.
3. Violet Cedar Paper.

Mimosa Cashmere Amber is the best warm fourth direction. Tuberose Night Lacquer should remain an exploratory contrast study until a small blind comparison earns it.

## Shared Trial Contract

- Use two coded, carrier-matched, constant-total arms.
- Change one relational variable at a time.
- Prepare a labeled working dilution for any direct raw dose below 10 µL.
- Convert the eventual dose design to ppm and screen ODT/OAV before compounding.
- Evaluate at opening, 30 minutes, 2 hours, and 4 hours.
- Record floral recognition, takeover, transition continuity, and liking separately.
- Liffarome 10% w/w and Cis-3-Hexenyl Salicylate 20% w/w remain outside the first builds because their carriers are unresolved and neither should be used as generic green decoration.
